# Trivy ignore list — every entry here must be justified and reviewed.
# Do NOT add an entry to silence a finding without a documented reason.
#
# Both CVEs below live in the SAME deeply-nested, build-time-only
# dependency chain used by the frontend asset bundler:
#
#   @vue/component-compiler -> postcss-modules-sync -> generic-names -> loader-utils@0.2.x -> json5@0.5.x
#
# Justification (applies to both CVEs):
#   - This chain is exercised only by `yarn build` / `bench build`, compiling
#     this repo's own .vue/.css source files at image-build time. It never
#     runs in the deployed application and never parses untrusted,
#     user-controlled input (the classic exploit vector for both CVEs is
#     parsing attacker-supplied JSON/query strings at runtime).
#   - `generic-names`/`postcss-modules-sync` pin the ancient 0.2.x
#     `loader-utils` range (`^0.2.16`) directly in their own package.json,
#     so a compatible fix cannot be pulled in via caret-range bump or a
#     scoped `resolutions` override without forcing a cross-major upgrade
#     (0.2 -> 1.x/2.x) of an API that changed significantly between majors.
#     That needs an upstream-package swap/removal, not a one-line dependency
#     bump — tracked as follow-up, out of scope for this CI-gate fix.
#   - Cheaply-fixable sibling CVEs in the same yarn.lock (ws, lodash,
#     shell-quote, tmp, launch-editor) were bumped in this same change via
#     `resolutions` / direct version bumps; these two are the only holdouts.

# json5: Prototype Pollution in JSON5 via Parse Method (transitive, build-time only, see above)
CVE-2022-46175

# loader-utils: prototype pollution in parseQuery.js (transitive, build-time only, see above)
CVE-2022-37601
